Package: code-on-incus
Architecture: any
# Bugs go to GitHub, not Launchpad's tracker and not the Maintainer's inbox.
# The XB- prefix is required: dpkg rejects a bare "Bugs:" here ("unknown
# information field") and only XB-* fields propagate into the binary control,
# where it lands as "Bugs:" for reportbug/apt to surface.
XB-Bugs: https://github.com/mensfeld/code-on-incus/issues
# libsystemd0 is NOT redundant with ${shlibs:Depends}: the NFT monitor reads the
# journal via go-systemd's sdjournal, which dlopen()s libsystemd.so.0 at runtime
# instead of linking it. dpkg-shlibdeps only sees link-time NEEDED entries, so it
# cannot infer this dependency and it has to be declared by hand.
# sudo is a hard dependency, not a convenience: the default network mode is
# "restricted", whose setup fails closed when passwordless `sudo -n nft` is
# unavailable, and that error aborts session setup outright. Running without
# sudo requires deliberately turning the isolation off ([network] mode = "open"
# or use_sudo = false), so a default-configured install needs it present.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libsystemd0,
         libcap2-bin,
         incus,
         sudo
# git is not required to start a session, but without it the host git-identity
# read comes back empty and COI keeps the fail-closed user.useConfigOnly guard,
# so commits inside the container fail; `[git] name`/`email` recovers that
# without the package. Its only other user is `coi update patterns` — threat
# detection falls back to a compiled-in pattern set.
Recommends: git
# coi never executes an OpenSSH binary. Agent forwarding just reads
# $SSH_AUTH_SOCK and proxies that socket into the container, so openssh-client
# is only relevant as the thing that would provide the agent in the first place.
Suggests: openssh-client
Description: isolated machines for AI coding agents, with active defense
 Code on Incus (the "coi" command) gives each AI coding agent its own machine:
 a full Incus system container with root access, systemd, Docker, and the
 ability to install anything. Agents work as they would on a real server -
 run services, manage packages, use cron - without touching the host.
 .
 Host credentials (SSH keys, environment variables, Git tokens) are never
 exposed to the agent unless explicitly mounted. coi watches each container
 and can pause or kill it automatically on suspicious activity such as reverse
 shells, credential scanning, or data exfiltration.
